Yes. An Alexandroff space (a topological space in which arbitrary intersections of open sets are open) is exactly characterized by the property that every point has a minimal open neighborhood.

## Proof

We prove both implications.

### (⇒) Alexandroff ⟹ every point has a minimal open neighborhood.

Let $X$ be an Alexandroff space and let $x \in X$. Consider the family
$$\mathcal{U}_x = \{ U \subseteq X : U \text{ is open and } x \in U \}$$
of all open neighborhoods of $x$. This family is nonempty (it contains $X$). Define
$$U_x = \bigcap_{U \in \mathcal{U}_x} U.$$
Since $X$ is Alexandroff, **arbitrary** intersections of open sets are open, so $U_x$ is open. Clearly $x \in U_x$. Moreover, $U_x \subseteq U$ for every $U \in \mathcal{U}_x$ by construction. Hence $U_x$ is an open neighborhood of $x$ contained in every open neighborhood of $x$, i.e., $U_x$ is the **minimal open neighborhood** of $x$.

### (⇐) Every point has a minimal open neighborhood ⟹ Alexandroff.

Suppose every point $x \in X$ has a minimal open neighborhood; denote it by $U_x$. We show that arbitrary intersections of open sets are open.

Let $\{V_\alpha\}_{\alpha \in A}$ be any family of open sets and set $V = \bigcap_{\alpha \in A} V_\alpha$. We claim $V$ is open.

For each $x \in V$, we have $x \in V_\alpha$ for every $\alpha \in A$, so each $V_\alpha$ is an open neighborhood of $x$. Since $U_x$ is the minimal open neighborhood of $x$, we have $U_x \subseteq V_\alpha$ for every $\alpha$. Therefore
$$U_x \subseteq \bigcap_{\alpha \in A} V_\alpha = V.$$
Since $x \in U_x \subseteq V$ for every $x \in V$, we can write
$$V = \bigcup_{x \in V} U_x.$$
Each $U_x$ is open, so $V$ is a union of open sets, hence open.

Thus arbitrary intersections of open sets are open, which is precisely the Alexandroff property. $\blacksquare$

